  Abstract                 = {Today, most developers work in parallel inside private workspaces to ensure stability during programming, but this provokes isolation from what co-workers are doing. Isolation may result in conflicts, which must be detected as early as possible to avoid bugs and expensive rework. Currently, frequent integration and awareness are used for early conflict detection. Frequent integration detects actual conflicts using automated builds, although only when merging checked in changes. Awareness informs about ongoing changes in private workspaces, however developers must find actual conflicts by themselves. This paper proposes the novel concept of real-time integration. This automates the detection of actual conflicts emerging during programming, involving checked in and ongoing changes, and affecting two or more developers. Cooperative resolution of conflicts is explicitly supported by sharing fine-grained changes among private workspaces. Software versions will also improve quality as integration builds are run before checking in. Ã‚Â© 2010 ACM.},
